at are in approved or  emergency approved  indications when you look at the list  though of those that are actually  available to be put into people's arms  as of today  you get the list that is here and that  list that you see  is of essentially seven different  vaccine  candidates they're summarized here and i  won't go into all of them  into any specific details because that's  beyond the purview of this presentation  today  but what i would like to address is  where i see some of the gaps in these  vaccines  so right now there's a couple of things  that i've highlighted  in this table that show where i think we  still have  some miles to go in terms of our ideal  vaccine for covit 19 and for future  pandemics  first you can see the two rna vaccines  these are really some of the lead  vaccine candidates  here in the united states they include  the pfizer and the modernity vaccines  these are mrna vaccines that are  delivered in a  butterball or a fat particle known as a  lipid nanoparticle  these vaccines have a two dose schedule  typically several weeks apart  and they are distinguished from each  other by certainly the pfizer vaccine  being  needed to be stored at deep cold  temperatures minus 20 degrees celsius  but they're also distinguished by the  fact that they're both close to 95  effective on the other hand there are  other  technologies apart from rna that are  also in vaccines that you can get around  the world  most notably here in the united states  are the johnson and johnson vaccines  these vaccines are based on a human  adenovirus platform and they're notable  for  single dose regimens the need for less  deep colds which is simple refrigeration  as for storage  and an efficacy that may not be as high  as the rna vaccines but certainly an  efficacy that would be enviable  for a seasonal flu vaccine the rest of  the list you can see is rounded out by  candidates which are  much more widely available in the rest  of the world these include vaccines that  are based on adenovirus  platforms and inactivated viruses the  distinguishing characteristics of these  vaccines are that many have multiple  dose regimens  many of them are required to have deep  cold storage  and at least two of them have efficacy  numbers which  are lower than has been reported  certainly for the rna vaccines  that we're using around the world so  taken together  where i think we still have some gaps  are in three  key areas these three key areas are in  access durability and breadth  in terms of access as i've mentioned in  the previous slide the requirement to  keep vaccines deeply cold  and even at refrigerated temperatures  put them out of reach  for many people around the world here in  the united states we often think that  that doesn't apply to us  that you know keeping a vaccine cold  shouldn't be an issue  but i'll show you in subsequent slides  it is an issue here in the united states  and i believe  continue to be as well as in places  where resources are extremely limited  and the cold chain is very fragile  on top of that we have issues of  durability so i'll show you some data  later in the presentation to show you  how long we model the current rna  vaccines  will protect individuals and there are  limits to that right now the current  guidance from the federal government in  the united states is that  the durability of protection is likely  about six months  could be longer but that's right now  what we're willing to say  that could be a problem for a virus that  shows many signs of becoming endemic  finally we have issues with breadth so  really anyone who's watching the news  today is constantly seeing messages  about these vaccine these viral variants  which  may reduce the efficacy of some of the  vaccines  that are currently being used and so we  really need vaccines that will address  all three of these issues access  durability and breadth  and the good news is that i think we  have technology now that can  can do just that so going into  a little bit more detail about access so  there have been  significant acts challenges to access  here in the united states  and that has been most uh represented by  what we're calling vaccine wastage so  what we know is that there's close to  100  000 doses of covid vaccines that have  been wasted within the first several  months of rollout  here in the united states despite the  fact that most people in this country  still cannot access a coven vaccine if  they want it  here on the right you can see some of  the press clippings but  as you know when a vaccine is brought to  a vaccination center and we have many of  these mass vaccination centers that have  sprung up  around around the united states when a  vaccine is brought to those centers  it must be used immediately when it is  thawed it can't be restored and so what  that means is that there is this rush  to use these vaccines that have been  brought to the vaccination centers and  if individuals don't show up for their  vaccines  it means that those doses get wasted so  here in the united states that's been a  significant problem  and one that's not getting a lot of  attention because we don't routinely  track the number of vaccines that are  wasted or have to be discarded  around the rest of the world though this  is an even greater problem so  in one study that was done a few years  ago it was estimated that 72 percent of  clinics in africa have  unreliable access to electricity oh half  of freeze-dried and  25 of liquid vaccines these are just  have to be stored under simple  refrigeration  are wasted um due to um due to  challenges in maintaining this cold  chain and deep cold chains are really  infusible  in regions where billions of people on  this globe live  there's some photographs on the right on  the top right you'll see  measles vaccines being delivered on  coolers  here on the backs of bikes  uh in the democratic republic of congo  and you can see that that is a real  challenge  and the uh slide below that the picture  below that is a  medicine freezer in nairobi uh a picture  that i took a few years ago  showing that even within these freezers  um  there are often are challenges due to  the constant electricity surges  in maintaining these cold temperatures  and with the frosting in the fridges  so really we need a solution that will  bring a vaccine that is stable at room  temperature  to get the billions of people on this  planet protected against  sars cov2 there are  important things that we need to think  about in terms of durability so in terms  of durability  we don't know the current duration of  protection the current recommendations  as i said are that these vaccines may be  protective for  six months but what we do know  and i'll show you some of these data  later are that um  that the levels of antibody titers  especially neutralizing antibodies which  we think are probably one of the better  correlates of protection  to many of our vaccines wane and so  we know that they exceed levels of  convalescent plasma for three months  but beyond that we're not sure so  based on some of the modeling data i'll  show you later we have reasons to be  concerned that the current vaccines that  we have  will not be durable certainly for more  than a year  and beyond that may require constant  boosting or  annual revaccination and given the  challenges that we've had even just with  a single  dose roll out it would be much more  ideal if we had a coveted vaccine  that could be durable for for decades  going to the next slide we also um  uh so so this these are data that as i  mentioned suggest  that um that the sars cov2 could be some  become rather than a seasonal  respiratory virus an endemic one so  we've already seen the virus  persist over two respiratory virus  seasons  and again there are many experts who  think that these pandemic coronaviruses  will circulate  for long periods of time the other  reason that i think we need to be  concerned is that we know that the level  of antibodies that we need to remain  protected against these viral variants  is higher and that if antibody titers  wane  from when you first become vaccinated  those waning titers may expose you  to greater risk from the viral variants  than even to the wild type and so the  vaccine could be even less durable  against variants should they emerge  the the final important feature that  we'll talk  about today that i think represents an  unmet need in current vaccines are the  breadth of protection  so i just mentioned the viral variants  and the  good news is that data suggests that all  the current vaccines  that are available in the united states  retain efficacy  against the coronaviruses that are of  different strains from the ones that  we're seeing here whether it's the uk  the south african brazilian variants and  so what you can see here is that on the  slide on the right  that these vaccines do retain some  efficacy against the viral variants but  that it is reduced  and that again does the combination of  reduced efficacy against the viral  variants  plus weaning antibody titers with the  time since vaccination  do those two factors together leave  people particularly vulnerable  as the as the epidemic  persists and progresses so with all of  this this hasn't been  bright cheery news and so rather what  i'd like to do is now turn to  what we think are tractable solutions  that could exist today  and be useful for protecting individuals  against covid19  and it's the severe effects of that  clinical disease  so the first technology that i'll talk  about is our thermostable rna  vaccines and the second technology that  i'll talk about are our vaccine  adjuvants so let me start with our  thermostable rna vaccine  so this is a table that i put together  that addresses what i think are the gaps  in current rna vaccines many of which  i've already spoken about but this goes  into it in more detail  and more than that how idris rna  vaccines address these current  limitations  so first on the left we've talked about  storage and distribution  and the need for the deep cold chain for  at least one of the two rna vaccines  so what we'll talk about today is that  our rna vaccines  are delivered with a novel delivery  vehicle that we call a nanostructured  lipid carrier  this nanostructured lipid carrier allows  the  vaccine to be biophilized or  freeze-dried and in so doing  it allows it to have up to one year of  stability at room temperature which we  think is a major advance  next in terms of potency our rna  platform is what we call  self-amplifying and self-adjuvanting  these two factors mean that we get  antibody titers after the first dose  that are higher  than after a multi-dose prime boost  regimen  so we think that the potency of these  vaccines may also be greater  with that greater potency we think we'll  have a greater duration of immunity  because it will take longer for the  antibody titers to fall  and finally we will have a very  adaptable platform and the reason for  that is that our  platform has advantages in terms of how  it's manufactured  that make it much more suitable to viral  variants and in that i'll tell you my  story about jelly donuts so with that  on the next slide we see um the the  basic design of our rna platform  is an alpha virus rna replicon based on  the  venezuelan equine encephalitis virus and  what we do is we knock out the  structural proteins from that alpha  virus  and replace them with the genes that  we're interested in to put into our  into our vaccine the this alpha virus  is delivered into the cell effectively  by our nanostructured lipid carrier  and once it's in the cell in the  cytoplasm it undergoes  a limited number of cycles of  replication so this is different than  mrna vaccines and in those 10 or 12  cycles of replication that the virus  goes through  it amplifies the target proteins at the  same time  the nanostructured lipid carrier  contains an adjuvant and that adjuvant  leads to more potent vaccine responses  so this is novel technology but what's  even equally as  novel is the way we deliver it so i want  to tell you about my tale of two donuts  i find when i talk about the story this  way most people  love and know donuts and so this really  resonates with folks  so the current vaccines um the moderna  and the pfizer biontech vaccines are  delivered by  this lipid nanoparticle and to me that  is like a jelly doughnut  so what your what you have is your  flavor of the doughnut in this case a  raspberry doughnut the raspberry  represents the strain of the virus  you're trying to  protect against so the raspberry in this  example  is the wild-type version of sars-ko v2  and the donut is the fat ball  the lipid nanoparticle that delivers it  to the cell what this means is that  every time you make this vaccine  you have to put the rna that you're  interested in  inside the the fat ball the lipid  nanoparticle and manufacture it in total  each time you want to have a vaccine  ready  on the other hand ours you can think of  more like a glazed donut that has  sprinkles on it  so in our case we can make billions of  glazed donuts  keep them in simple storage and  stockpile them  and then each time there is a new viral  variant or  a a new pandemic we simply change the  sprinkles so instead of rainbow  sprinkles we may use chocolate sprinkles  or we may use vanilla sprinkles  and that gives us incredible flexibility  to not have to re-manufacture the entire  vaccine platform  each time there's a new viral variant or  each time there's a new strain  it also means that we can vaccinate in a  multivalent fashion much like our  seasonal  influenza vaccine contains three or four  different strains that are circulating  we can decorate three or four  different strains on to the surface of  our donut  and protect against multiple coronavirus  strains  so in more technical terms the  comparison between the two delivery  vehicles is shown here  on the left you see that the rna is  shoved inside the lipid nanoparticle on  the right you see our nanostructured  lipid carriers  they our nlcs have enhanced stability  and they're as i'll show you on the next  slide are delivered more efficiently  into the cell  they have the rna decorated on the  outside and yet even though it's on the  outside it remains stable at room  temperature for a year  it's attracted to this butter ball by uh  electrostatic charges so the rna is  negatively charged and the phosphate  groups  are positively charged and this means  that we can stockpile the nanostructured  lipid carrier  and quickly manufacture the rna to suit  and this means that we're both scalable  and widely  ready for the next pandemic and then  within that core  of this butterball is a lipid core  but that includes squalene which many of  you may know  is used currently as a vaccine adjuvant  for many different types of vaccines but  is a potent vaccine adjuvant that we  think  adds potency to this platform the data  which are pre-published on bioarchives  and you can see them here  undergoing review and a peer-reviewed  journal show that  really the experiments on the left show  that the  vaccine construct  resists rna degradation uh with rna's  challenge  for up to eight months at room  temperature and up to two years at  uh at four degrees c and that room  temperature is 25 degrees celsius and  the graph that's next to  in the middle here it shows the biologic  activity  of the vaccine looking at protein  expression  after it has been kept at room  temperature or refrigerated temperatures  refrigerated a simple refrigeration for  21 months room temperature on the  counter for eight months  so it really retains activity just like  the freshly made vaccine  and then one of the things that we think  is adding to the potency is on the graph  on the right  you can see that generally we get about  one log  higher production of protein  when we deliver the same rna via the  nanostructured lipid carrier compared to  the lipid nanoparticle so we think we  have a potent and thermostable delivery  vehicle  which we pair together with our  our rna replicon and together we think  that this is a  very important solution to the  rna vaccine problem and getting rna  vaccines to every corner of the globe  to every person who needs one this  technology right now  has completed its most of its  pre-clinical studies  it's working its way through toxicology  studies and we hope to start a phase 1  clinical trial  by quarter 4 of this year  i want to turn now to our potent vaccine  adjuvants  so by way of background many people may  know that adjuvants  are molecules that have been designed to  interact with the innate immune system  and their purpose is to increase the  immune response against an infectious  disease or a cancer  we've put adjuvants in vaccines for  close to 100 years  we first started putting heavy metals in  vaccines  at the beginning of the 1900s and  we recognized that by doing so these  heavy metals  caused an inflammatory response that  increased the efficacy of different  vaccines  but we're really in the golden age of  adjuvants now and i would say that  that's been heralded by the approval of  i would say one of the first modern  adjuvant formulations in 2017  and this is a system that includes a  toll-like receptor agonist  a tlr4 agonist known as mpl along with  other excipients and carrier molecules  and so  that first was included in the vaccine  shingrix  that took a vaccine for a that was  pre-ex  that previously vaccines for shingles  were about 50  effective and got less effective as you  got older and more vulnerable ironically  to shingles  but it took that vaccine and changed it  and  changed both the construct of the  vaccine and the adjuvant that was used  and in adding an adjuvant to a  glycoprotein vaccine that vaccine became  close to 98 effective including in  people over the age of 80.  so really that hurled at a new age of  adjuvants  and now we recognize that this  combination of a toll-like receptor  agonist  in different formulations allows for  highly potent vaccines  these these adjuvants can be  freeze-dried and stored at room  temperature for long periods of  time and what we know is that they allow  for the protection of vulnerable  populations through a number of  mechanisms  they allow for broader responses and  they allow for more durable responses  and i'll show you some data that's been  generated in the context of kovit and  these are data that are sort of hot off  the press  and now into pre-publication so first  here's a publication that you can see  the reference below  pre-publication again on bioarchive that  looks at the addition of an idria  adjuvant called 3m o52  alum that's been co-developed with 3m  this adjuvant when added to a sars cov2  rbd  ferritin nanoparticle leads to as you  can see here  about a one log higher titer of  neutralizing antibodies compared to  rna vaccines and you can see that it  does that both to the d614 g  sars cov2 variant as well as the b117  variant  on the right what you can see though is  very interestingly that the addition  of the adjuvant to the protein vaccine  leads to broad cross-strain protection  so  by adding the adjuvant you afford  protection against stars cov1 stars cov2  and two different strains of that  coronavirus so we're very encouraged by  this and we think that  the addition of a strong toll-like  receptor adjuvant  in this case atollic receptor 7-8  agonist  to a very good protein vaccine  could actually allow for cross-strain  protection  and more than that we think that this  protection will be durable  so this is also data that's been  pre-published now  again referenced below but what we've  compared is the use of  a similar adjuvant formulation this one  instead of with alum but with a  squarely a stable emulsion uh here s e  so 3m o5 2 se using that  that tlr-78 adjuvant  with a protein vaccine leads to  significantly higher  vac antibody titers both on the left you  can see total igg  and on the right you can see pseudovirus  neutralization titers  so higher than the rna vaccines and  non-human primates  but also as you can see on the uh on the  bottom right  that the duration of protection i mean  this is antibody titers as expressed by  ic50  really the the shape of the curve and  the decline in the curve as modeled out  with the blue uh line which is the  uh adjuvanted protein vaccine affords a  significantly longer protection  the time scale on the bottom is on the  farther most right time point of 72  weeks  so again modeling protection above that  that lower limit of protection for  you know about a year and a half and  that's  we think very impressive for um the  current state of  uh covid vaccines with the last few  moments i want to turn to something  that's completely different  that we're doing here at injury and so  what that is is we're also looking at  new ways to treat this infection  so we think that there are limitations  to current treatments for covid while  the  morbidity is certainly and the mortality  of covet has declined  what we are seeing is that of the  treatments that are currently  available we see a couple of limitations  one is that they may  may not have efficacy across all groups  of people  who are have covid19 disease what i mean  by that is that there are several  therapies that are most effective in  individuals with severe disease  or some in very early disease but few  for the full spectrum of patients  at the same time we worry that the  treatment of some of these  using some of these modalities may  render  patients more susceptible to future  covet infections by reducing immunity  to the virus in the context of treating  the acute infection  so we have lots of reasons to think that  natural killer cells which are an  important arm of the immune system  play a critical role in viral infections  what we know is that these cells traffic  to the lung  that they recognize virally infected  cells to become activated  that once they're activated they kill  the target cell but then they also  increa secrete interferon gamma and tnf  alpha  that allows the recruitment and priming  of t cells so while they both kill the  virus  and the virally infected cells they also  may help to establish a more effective  cytokine milieu  and in so doing lead to more effective  treatment and  future protection against the virus we  currently are  leading a phase one phase two  clinical trial uh of an nk cell product  known as sync01 that is produced by a  company in new jersey called cellularity  and what we're looking to investigate is  whether the treatment with sync01  leads to better clinical outcomes as  well as a number of translational  outcomes  in patients with moderate covid19  infection  so that trial is currently ongoing it's  a two-phase study with a phase one  portion that is  not randomized to look at safety and  tolerability  and a phase two portion that is  randomized to really look at efficacy  and here you can see some of the data  that we'll be presenting over the next  several weeks  um it's a little too small to to really  see well here but  of the first six participants that have  been treated to date we've seen  responses and five of those six  participants um and  of those participants they've cleared  their virus  they have had clinical improvements in  radiography over a very short time  period  as well as an assessment of clinical  benefit by their treating clinicians  so we're excited about this therapy  we're excited to complete this ongoing  clinical trial and we hope that this  will add to our overall knowledge  of how we deal with this pandemic most  excitingly these nk cells were off the  shelf  they are not specific to covid19 it  could be very useful for future pandemic  respiratory viral infections  so in summary what i'd like to end with  is are these conclusions  that we've made amazing progress um as a  as a scientific community in in speeding  several coveted vaccines through the  clinical development pipeline i mean to  think of a vaccine  going from initial conception to  emergency approval  or emergency use authorization in just  11 months  is nothing short of remarkable however  we still  the majority of the population around  the world and even here in the united  states  lack access to these vaccines and what  we need to do  is make sure that everyone around the  world has access to these technologies  and that they have access to  technologies that  are durable and have protect against a  broad range of viruses  so with that we hope that these tools  and technologies our thermostable rna  vaccines  our adjuvanted protein vaccines and even  some of these nk cell therapies  may lead to future successes against  this and other pandemic viruses  um our institute here in seattle uh  is we're 28 years old um we are a small  research institute but a potent one  with a budget of about 25 million  dollars in this fiscal year  we have about 65 employees almost all of  whom are in scientific roles  and we work on next generation vaccines  we work on global health solutions as  i've just described  and we have incredible manufacturing  capacities here in seattle that  serve as a resource for academic  nonprofit and startup biotech  around the world so with that i thank  you for your time and your attention  today  my contact information is here and i'm  always  excited and eager to talk to  collaborators around the world  about the work that we do here and the  work that all of you are doing  to help end this pandemic and prevent  future ones  thanks for your time today

